# Credentials and configuration for the triage harness.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill it in:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#     $EDITOR .env
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a      # export everything in it into this shell
#
# The harness does NOT parse this file itself. It reads the process environment and
# nothing else, so a key loaded this way is indistinguishable from one exported by hand
# and can never be written to the SQLite state, logged, or committed.
#
# `.env` is git-ignored. Do not move credentials into `config/harness.yaml`: that file is
# tracked, and only `${VAR}` references in it are resolved from the environment.
#
# The harness does not read this file by itself. Point it at the file explicitly, which
# works identically in every shell:
#
#     harness --env-file .env --config config/deepseek.yaml run
#
# If you would rather export it the shell way, note that `set -a; . ./.env; set +a` is
# bash and zsh only — in fish it is three separate errors, because fish has no allexport
# and a sourced file cannot contain a bare KEY=value. The fish equivalent, with the
# regexes in single quotes so fish does not try to expand the `$`:
#
#     for line in (grep -vE '^\s*(#|$)' .env)
#         set -gx (string split -m1 = -- $line)
#     end
#
# Existing environment variables always win, so an inline override still works:
#     DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-other harness --env-file .env run

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub — required for `harness run` (Dependabot alerts) and for `scan-public`
# (the git tree, structure_hash, and the checkout clone).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option A: a personal access token. Simplest, and enough for testing.
#   classic PAT needs:   repo, read:org, security_events
#   fine-grained PAT needs: Contents (read), Dependabot alerts (read)
GH_TOKEN=

# Option B: a GitHub App. Preferred for a fleet; takes precedence when all three are set.
# The installation token they mint is short-lived, which is the point.
GH_APP_ID=
GH_INSTALLATION_ID=
GH_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=          # path to the .pem, never the key itself

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model providers — required only for the agent stages (recon, judgment, validation).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which variable you need is decided by `models.*.provider` in the config you pass:
#   provider: anthropic  ->  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   provider: openai     ->  OPENAI_API_KEY
#   provider: deepseek   ->  DEEPSEEK_API_KEY   (OpenAI-compatible endpoint)
#
# Each vendor's key is its own. There is no fallback between them: pointing one vendor's
# key at another vendor's endpoint is a misroute only noticed on the invoice.
#
# Run `harness models` to see which model ids a key can actually call. Tier names are
# marketing; the identifier is what the API accepts.
#
# Leave every key blank and the deterministic half still runs at $0.00:
#   harness scan-public --repo <owner/name> --agents off
#   python eval/run_eval.py --allow-synthetic
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
